---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert content strategist, content repurposing specialist, social media strategist and editorial planner.

TASK:
Transform one existing piece of content into multiple useful content assets for different platforms, formats, audiences and stages of the customer journey.

The goal is NOT to simply shorten or copy the original content.

Each repurposed asset should:
- preserve the original meaning
- extract the strongest ideas
- adapt naturally to the selected platform
- provide value independently
- match the target audience
- support the stated marketing or creator objective
- avoid unnecessary repetition
- avoid inventing facts that are not present in the source

ORIGINAL CONTENT:
[Paste the article, video transcript, podcast transcript, newsletter, guide, report, social post, webinar notes or other source content.]

CONTENT TYPE:
[Article / video / podcast / guide / report / newsletter / social post / webinar / other.]

ORIGINAL PURPOSE:
[Education / awareness / lead generation / sales / engagement / authority / other.]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who should the repurposed content reach?]

PRIMARY OBJECTIVE:
[Reach / engagement / traffic / leads / sales / education / authority / community / other.]

PLATFORMS:
[Facebook / Instagram / LinkedIn / TikTok / YouTube / X / email / blog / other.]

FORMATS NEEDED:
[Reels / Shorts / posts / carousels / threads / emails / articles / captions / scripts / other.]

BRAND / CREATOR VOICE:
[Professional / practical / friendly / educational / conversational / premium / energetic / other.]

CTA:
[Follow / comment / save / share / visit / download / subscribe / buy / enquire / other.]

CONTENT PILLARS:
[Optional.]

KEY MESSAGE THAT MUST BE PRESERVED:
[Optional.]

FACTS / CLAIMS THAT MUST NOT CHANGE:
[Optional.]

WORDS / TOPICS TO AVOID:
[Optional.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

CONTENT REPURPOSING REQUIREMENTS:

1. ANALYZE THE ORIGINAL CONTENT FIRST

2. IDENTIFY THE CORE MESSAGE

3. IDENTIFY THE MAIN SUPPORTING IDEAS

4. IDENTIFY USEFUL FACTS

5. IDENTIFY EXAMPLES

6. IDENTIFY STORIES OR EXPERIENCES

7. IDENTIFY ACTIONABLE STEPS

8. IDENTIFY QUESTIONS ANSWERED BY THE CONTENT

9. IDENTIFY STRONG OPINIONS OR INSIGHTS

10. IDENTIFY QUOTABLE IDEAS
Do not create fabricated quotations.

11. DISTINGUISH SOURCE FACTS FROM INTERPRETATION

12. DO NOT INVENT NEW FACTS

13. DO NOT INVENT STATISTICS

14. DO NOT INVENT RESEARCH

15. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER RESULTS

16. DO NOT INVENT TESTIMONIALS

17. DO NOT INVENT EXPERT QUOTES

18. DO NOT CHANGE IMPORTANT NUMBERS

19. DO NOT ALTER TECHNICAL MEANING FOR A BETTER HOOK

20. PRESERVE IMPORTANT QUALIFIERS

21. IDENTIFY CONTENT ATOMS
Break the source into reusable units such as:
Insight
Tip
Mistake
Question
Example
Step
Statistic
Story
Comparison
Checklist
Definition
Warning
Myth
Framework

22. DO NOT TREAT EVERY SENTENCE AS A SEPARATE CONTENT IDEA

23. PRIORITIZE IDEAS THAT CAN STAND ALONE

24. CREATE MULTIPLE CONTENT ANGLES

25. POSSIBLE ANGLES:
Educational
Problem-led
Mistake-led
How-to
Checklist
Question
Myth versus reality
Behind the scenes
Opinion
Story
Comparison
Quick tip
Common misconception
Step-by-step
FAQ

26. EACH ANGLE SHOULD ADD A DIFFERENT REASON TO ENGAGE

27. DO NOT CREATE TEN POSTS THAT SAY THE SAME THING

28. ADAPT CONTENT TO PLATFORM BEHAVIOUR

29. FACEBOOK MODE
Consider:
Useful feed posts
Short videos
Reels
Discussion questions
Shareable tips
Community-oriented content

30. INSTAGRAM MODE
Consider:
Reels
Carousels
Stories
Visual tips
Saveable educational content

31. LINKEDIN MODE
Consider:
Professional insights
Operational lessons
Frameworks
Case-style examples
Business observations
Educational posts

32. TIKTOK MODE
Consider:
Immediate hooks
Short explanations
Demonstrations
Fast tips
Problem-first videos
Natural spoken language

33. YOUTUBE SHORTS MODE
Create:
Hook
Fast value
Clear progression
Payoff
Simple CTA

34. YOUTUBE LONG-FORM MODE
Expand only when the source contains enough substance.

35. EMAIL MODE
Create:
Subject
Preview text
Opening
Main value
CTA

36. BLOG MODE
Create a useful article structure rather than simply expanding a short social post with filler.

37. ADAPT LENGTH TO PLATFORM

38. DO NOT USE IDENTICAL COPY ACROSS EVERY PLATFORM

39. PRESERVE BRAND VOICE ACROSS FORMATS

40. ADAPT DELIVERY WITHOUT CHANGING IDENTITY

41. CREATE PLATFORM-NATIVE HOOKS

42. HOOKS SHOULD BE TRUTHFUL

43. DO NOT CREATE CLICKBAIT THAT THE CONTENT DOES NOT DELIVER

44. POSSIBLE HOOK STRUCTURES:
Question
Problem
Mistake
Unexpected insight
Useful result
Specific situation
Direct promise of information

45. DO NOT USE FALSE URGENCY

46. DO NOT USE FALSE SCARCITY

47. CREATE SHORT-FORM VIDEO AS AN ACTUAL SCRIPT

48. SHORT-FORM SCRIPT SHOULD INCLUDE:
Opening hook
Visual direction
Voice-over or spoken line
Main value
Payoff
CTA

49. KEEP SHORT-FORM VIDEOS FOCUSED ON ONE MAIN IDEA

50. DO NOT FORCE AN ENTIRE ARTICLE INTO ONE REEL

51. CREATE CAROUSELS AS A SEQUENCE

52. CAROUSEL STRUCTURE MAY INCLUDE:
Slide 1 — Hook
Slide 2 — Context
Slides 3–6 — Main value
Final slide — Summary / CTA

53. KEEP EACH SLIDE FOCUSED

54. DO NOT PUT PARAGRAPHS OF TEXT ON EACH SLIDE

55. CREATE SOCIAL POSTS THAT WORK WITHOUT REQUIRING THE ORIGINAL CONTENT

56. PROVIDE ENOUGH CONTEXT FOR A NEW VIEWER

57. CREATE CAPTIONS THAT COMPLEMENT THE VISUAL

58. DO NOT SIMPLY TRANSCRIBE THE VIDEO INTO THE CAPTION

59. CREATE EMAIL CONTENT THAT HAS A CLEAR PURPOSE

60. DO NOT TURN EVERY SOURCE INTO A SALES EMAIL

61. CREATE CONTENT FOR DIFFERENT FUNNEL STAGES WHEN RELEVANT

62. AWARENESS CONTENT:
Problem
Insight
Education
Question

63. CONSIDERATION CONTENT:
How-to
Comparison
Framework
Demonstration

64. CONVERSION CONTENT:
Use case
Proof
Offer
Product explanation

65. RETENTION CONTENT:
Tips
Advanced usage
FAQ
New use case

66. ONLY USE CONVERSION CONTENT WHEN THE SOURCE AND BUSINESS CONTEXT SUPPORT IT

67. CREATE CONTENT FOR DIFFERENT AUDIENCE KNOWLEDGE LEVELS

68. BEGINNER VERSION
Explain simply.

69. INTERMEDIATE VERSION
Add process and practical detail.

70. ADVANCED VERSION
Focus on nuance, trade-offs and deeper application.

71. DO NOT DUMB DOWN TECHNICAL CONTENT UNTIL IT BECOMES INCORRECT

72. CREATE CONTENT SERIES WHEN THE SOURCE HAS ENOUGH DEPTH

73. SERIES SHOULD HAVE A LOGICAL THEME

74. DO NOT CREATE A SERIES FROM THIN CONTENT JUST TO INCREASE POST COUNT

75. CREATE EVERGREEN ASSETS

76. IDENTIFY CONTENT THAT CAN REMAIN USEFUL OVER TIME

77. IDENTIFY TIME-SENSITIVE CONTENT

78. FLAG INFORMATION THAT MAY NEED UPDATING BEFORE REUSE

79. IDENTIFY HIGH-VALUE REPURPOSING OPPORTUNITIES

80. PRIORITIZE BASED ON:
Audience relevance
Platform fit
Information value
Production effort
Reuse potential

81. CREATE LOW-EFFORT OPTIONS

82. EXAMPLES:
Text post
Quote-style insight
FAQ
Checklist
Short email
Caption

83. CREATE MEDIUM-EFFORT OPTIONS

84. EXAMPLES:
Carousel
Short-form video
Visual guide
Newsletter

85. CREATE HIGHER-EFFORT OPTIONS ONLY WHEN JUSTIFIED

86. EXAMPLES:
Long-form video
Detailed article
Webinar
Downloadable guide

87. CREATE A CONTENT REUSE CHAIN

88. EXAMPLE:
Long article
→ LinkedIn post
→ Carousel
→ Reel
→ Email
→ FAQ
→ Short tip

89. DO NOT CREATE A CHAIN THAT LOSES THE ORIGINAL MESSAGE

90. CREATE INTERNAL LINKING OPPORTUNITIES WHEN RELEVANT

91. EXAMPLE:
Social post → full guide
Email → tool
Short video → article
Article → template

92. ALIGN CTA WITH CONTENT INTENT

93. EDUCATIONAL CONTENT SHOULD NOT ALWAYS END WITH A HARD SALES CTA

94. POSSIBLE LOW-FRICTION CTAS:
Save this
Share this
Follow for more
Read the full guide
Try the tool

95. HIGHER-INTENT CTAS:
Download
Request quote
Book
Buy
Start trial

96. USE ONLY CTAS THAT ACTUALLY EXIST

97. CREATE CONTENT VARIATION

98. VARY:
Hook
Format
Depth
Perspective
Audience stage
Visual treatment

99. DO NOT VARY FACTS

100. CREATE VISUAL DIRECTIONS

101. POSSIBLE VISUALS:
Talking head
Screen recording
Product demonstration
Process footage
Diagram
Infographic
Carousel
Before/after when legitimate
B-roll
Static graphic

102. DO NOT INVENT VISUAL PROOF

103. CREATE B-ROLL IDEAS WHEN USEFUL

104. B-ROLL SHOULD SUPPORT THE MESSAGE

105. CREATE ON-SCREEN TEXT

106. KEEP ON-SCREEN TEXT SHORT

107. PRESERVE EXACT NUMBERS AND IMPORTANT TERMS

108. RECOMMEND MANUAL TEXT OVERLAY WHEN AI-GENERATED IMAGE TEXT ACCURACY IS CRITICAL

109. CREATE CONTENT TITLES

110. TITLES SHOULD BE SPECIFIC

111. AVOID GENERIC TITLES SUCH AS:
Top Tips
Amazing Advice
You Need to Know This

when a more informative title is possible.

112. CREATE SEO OPPORTUNITIES WHEN BLOG OR YOUTUBE CONTENT IS REQUESTED

113. IDENTIFY NATURAL SEARCH QUESTIONS

114. DO NOT INVENT SEARCH VOLUME

115. DO NOT KEYWORD STUFF

116. CREATE CONTENT CALENDAR OPTIONS

117. GROUP REPURPOSED ASSETS LOGICALLY

118. DO NOT PUBLISH NEAR-IDENTICAL CONTENT BACK-TO-BACK

119. SPACE RELATED IDEAS WHEN APPROPRIATE

120. CREATE CROSS-PLATFORM DISTRIBUTION ORDER

121. IDENTIFY THE BEST "SOURCE" ASSET

122. IDENTIFY THE BEST "DISCOVERY" ASSETS

123. IDENTIFY THE BEST "DEPTH" ASSETS

124. IDENTIFY THE BEST "CONVERSION" ASSETS

125. CREATE A PRODUCTION WORKFLOW

126. POSSIBLE WORKFLOW:
Extract
Select
Adapt
Produce
Review
Schedule
Measure
Reuse

127. REDUCE DUPLICATE PRODUCTION

128. REUSE RECORDINGS, VISUALS AND RESEARCH WHEN APPROPRIATE

129. CREATE A FACT-PRESERVATION CHECK

130. VERIFY:
Numbers
Names
Dates
Technical details
Claims
Quotes
Instructions

131. FLAG OUTDATED INFORMATION

132. FLAG CLAIMS THAT REQUIRE REVALIDATION

133. CREATE A BRAND-CONSISTENCY CHECK

134. VERIFY:
Tone
Terminology
Positioning
CTA
Visual direction

135. CREATE A PLATFORM-FIT CHECK

136. ASK:
Does this feel native to the platform?
Is the hook appropriate?
Is the length appropriate?
Does the format match user behaviour?

137. CREATE A DUPLICATION CHECK

138. IDENTIFY ASSETS THAT ARE TOO SIMILAR

139. MERGE OR DIFFERENTIATE THEM

140. CREATE PERFORMANCE LEARNING

141. IF HISTORICAL DATA IS PROVIDED
Use it to prioritize:
Topics
Hooks
Formats
Platforms

142. DO NOT INVENT PERFORMANCE DATA

143. CREATE TEST IDEAS

144. TEST ONE MEANINGFUL VARIABLE AT A TIME

145. POSSIBLE TESTS:
Hook
Format
Length
CTA
Opening visual

146. DO NOT GUARANTEE PERFORMANCE

147. CREATE A REPURPOSING PRIORITY

148. RANK ASSETS BY:
Expected relevance
Production effort
Strategic value

149. CREATE A MINIMUM-VIABLE REPURPOSING PLAN
For limited time or resources, identify the smallest useful set of assets.

150. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- original message is preserved
- important facts are unchanged
- no statistics were invented
- no quotes were fabricated
- content is adapted rather than merely copied
- each asset has a clear purpose
- formats fit their platforms
- hooks accurately represent the content
- short-form assets focus on one main idea
- carousels have logical progression
- captions complement visuals
- CTAs match content intent
- content is not unnecessarily repetitive
- evergreen and time-sensitive material are distinguished
- production effort is considered
- content can be reused efficiently
- platform-native differences are present
- brand voice remains consistent
- claims are proportionate to evidence
- the final plan is practical to execute

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. SOURCE CONTENT ANALYSIS
Provide:
Core message
Main ideas
Useful facts
Examples
Actionable steps
Important claims
Potentially outdated information

2. CONTENT ATOM LIBRARY
Extract reusable:
Insights
Tips
Questions
Mistakes
Steps
Examples
Frameworks
Definitions
Warnings

3. REPURPOSING STRATEGY
Provide:
Primary objective
Audience
Best platforms
Recommended formats
Content priorities

4. REPURPOSED CONTENT MATRIX

For each asset provide:
Asset title
Platform
Format
Content angle
Audience stage
Hook
Core message
CTA
Production effort

5. SHORT-FORM VIDEO CONTENT
For each recommended Reel / Short provide:
Hook
Visual
Script
On-screen text
CTA

6. SOCIAL POSTS
Write platform-appropriate posts.

7. CAROUSEL CONTENT
Provide:
Slide-by-slide structure
Caption
CTA

8. EMAIL CONTENT
When relevant provide:
Subject
Preview text
Body
CTA

9. LONG-FORM OPPORTUNITIES
Recommend:
Article
Newsletter
YouTube video
Guide

only when the source has enough depth.

10. CONTENT SERIES
Create a logical series if appropriate.

11. VISUAL & B-ROLL PLAN
Recommend practical supporting visuals.

12. CONTENT REUSE CHAIN
Show how major assets can feed smaller assets.

13. DISTRIBUTION PLAN
Recommend the order in which content should be published or distributed.

14. MINIMUM-VIABLE PLAN
If resources are limited, provide the 3–5 highest-value assets to create first.

15. FACT & CLAIM CHECK
List:
Fact / claim
Source status
Whether it can be reused safely
Anything requiring verification

16. DUPLICATION CHECK
Identify assets that are too similar and explain how to differentiate them.

17. TEST PLAN
Suggest useful content tests without guaranteeing performance.

18. FINAL PRODUCTION CHECKLIST
Provide the practical steps required to turn the plan into publishable content.

IMPORTANT:
- Preserve the original meaning.
- Do not invent facts, research, statistics, quotes, testimonials or results.
- Adapt content to each platform instead of copying identical text everywhere.
- Prioritize quality and usefulness over producing the largest possible number of assets.
- Keep short-form content focused.
- Match CTA to audience intent.
- Flag outdated or uncertain information before reuse.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Extract before rewriting",
    description:
      "Break the source into useful ideas, examples, questions, steps and insights before deciding which formats to create.",
  },
  {
    title: "Give each asset a purpose",
    description:
      "Use different pieces for discovery, education, engagement, consideration or conversion instead of publishing the same message repeatedly.",
  },
  {
    title: "Adapt to the platform",
    description:
      "Turn the same underlying insight into a native Reel, LinkedIn post, carousel, email or article rather than copying identical wording everywhere.",
  },
  {
    title: "Protect the original facts",
    description:
      "Keep numbers, claims, quotations and technical meaning consistent with the source and flag anything that needs updating or verification.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Source: An article explaining six areas to investigate when physical stock does not match system stock.",
    "Audience: Warehouse supervisors, inventory controllers and small-business operators.",
    "Objective: Educational reach and traffic to the full guide.",
    "Platforms: LinkedIn, Facebook and short-form video.",
    "CTA: Read the full guide.",
  ],

  output: [
    "LINKEDIN POST: Use the professional problem as the opening — 'Physical stock doesn't match the system. Where do you check first?' Then summarize the investigation sequence and link to the detailed guide.",
    "CAROUSEL: Slide 1 introduces the discrepancy problem. Slides 2–7 cover one investigation area each. Final slide directs readers to the complete checklist.",
    "SHORT VIDEO: Open with a stock-count discrepancy on screen, then quickly show receiving, picking, adjustments, transfers, damages and transaction history as investigation checkpoints.",
    "FACEBOOK POST: Use a practical checklist format designed for saving and sharing rather than copying the LinkedIn post word for word.",
    "FAQ ASSET: Turn common investigation questions into individual educational posts that can be published later.",
    "FACT CHECK: Preserve the original investigation steps and avoid claiming that checking these six areas will identify every possible inventory discrepancy.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Repurposing is not copying",
    description:
      "The underlying idea can stay consistent while the hook, depth, format, pacing and presentation change to suit each platform.",
  },
  {
    title: "One strong source can become a content system",
    description:
      "A useful guide, video or article can supply short videos, posts, carousels, FAQs and emails without requiring a completely new topic every time.",
  },
  {
    title: "More assets are not automatically better",
    description:
      "Prioritize the formats with the strongest audience and platform fit instead of stretching thin source material into unnecessary posts.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Content Repurposing Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Turn articles, videos, guides and other source content into platform-native posts, Reels, carousels, emails and reusable content assets."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="MARKETING & CREATORS"
    title="Content Repurposing"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Turn one strong piece of content into a practical multi-platform content system while preserving the original facts, message and brand voice."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Content repurposing",
      "Multi-platform",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Content creators",
      "Small businesses",
      "Social media",
      "Blog content",
      "Reels & Shorts",
      "Content marketing",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Create more useful content without starting from zero."
    introDescription="Extract the strongest ideas from an existing article, video, guide or other source and adapt them into platform-native assets with different purposes, formats and levels of depth."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn one inventory guide into a multi-platform content package."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Reuse the insight, not necessarily the exact content."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="One prompt, two KRIYANO categories"
    sidebarDescription="Content Repurposing supports both Marketing and Creators because the same workflow can help businesses distribute campaigns and creators build more content from their strongest source material."
    warningTitle="Repurposing should not distort the source"
    warningText="Recheck dates, statistics, quotations, product claims and technical information before republishing older content. Do not allow a stronger hook or shorter format to change the original meaning."
  />
</BaseLayout>